        If you want to use Allegro's standard file extension support (the extension is appended if not present), then see`<cdsroot>/share/pcb/text/fileops.txt`for a list of `t_ids`. Otherwise, if you always provide an extension, use the `TEMP` id.

        * Use`get_filename(p_port)` to obtain the name of the file.

        Args:
            t_id: File attribute id. This string must be one of the types in the Allegro PCB Editor system file`fileops.txt`. Examples are `ALLEGRO_LAYOUT_DB` for Allegro PCB Editor layouts with extension `brd`, `ALLEGRO_REPORT` for Allegro PCB Editor report files with extension `rpt`.
            t_name: String giving the name of the file to open.
            t_mode: Open mode. One of the following: `r`: read-only `w`: write, create if doesn't exist, truncate to zero length if exists `a`: open for writing, create if doesn't exist, go to end of file for appending if exists `f:`Flush file after each write. This can be slow on Windows if writing across the network. This is typically used if a process will take a long time and you would like to look at the file to see the progress. Example `"wf".` `b:`Open in binary mode. This only has effect on Windows. If file is ASCII, this has the effect for reading of not eliminating the carriage-returns (\\r) that are in DOS ASCII files. For writing, it does not add the carriage-returns when it sees a linefeed (writes it like a UNIX ASCII file). Example `"rb".` `s:`Allow spaces in the file or directory name. Currently, Allegro does not support this behavior. Setting this option is unsupported. Example `"rbs".`

        Returns:
            p_port: Port of the opened file. In the case of`t_mode` `="``r"`, the file must exist for successful completion.
            nil: File not found. Displays a confirmer giving the name of the file it could not find.

        Example:
            Opens a file`clip.txt` for writing.

            ```
            aPort = axlDMOpenFile("ALLEGRO_TEXT" "clip" "w")
            ```

